== Objects bridge pixels and symbols

The most consistent shift is toward object-centric state representations as the interface between perception and reasoning. Early systems like DSRL had to pull symbols out of pixels with fragile, task-specific methods @garnelo2016. Recent work treats the object list as a standard middle layer. OCAtari gives object-centric states for Atari games, which let people compare symbolic and neural agents on a common benchmark @delfosse2024oc. SCoBots adds a concept bottleneck. The agent first extracts objects, then computes relational concepts over them, like distances between objects. Only then does it pick an action with an interpretable policy over those concepts @delfosse2024. Because you can inspect every intermediate representation, you can see which concepts the policy leans on. You can remove or correct concepts that cause unintended behavior. The authors pitch this as a way to align agents with what their designers intended.

The big deal here is that it separates grounding from reasoning. Once you have objects, you can apply all the formalisms from @sec:bg-symbolic to them. The catch is that the quality of the whole system now rides on object extraction. That's easy in Atari and much harder in realistic visual scenes.

== From two-stage pipelines to end-to-end training

The second shift is about how you train the symbolic part. Earlier differentiable logic methods trained a neural agent first, then used that agent to guide the search for rules. NUDGE is one example. A pretrained neural policy acts as an oracle that picks promising candidate rules, and then those rule weights get optimized with a logic actor and a neural critic @delfosse2023. You get readable policies, but you need a good neural policy to start with.

More recent work trains the neural and symbolic parts together. INSIGHT learns a compact symbolic policy jointly with the perception module that feeds it, and it uses a language model to turn the learned policy into textual explanations @luo2024. BlendRL goes further. It keeps both kinds of policy in the final agent. A neural policy works over pixels and a logic policy works over objects, and a learned blending function mixes them. So the agent can lean on logic where reasoning matters and on the neural policy where fine-grained control matters @shindo2025. On modified versions of three Atari games, where a standard neural PPO agent scored zero, BlendRL still got high scores. That suggests the logic component carries the abstract structure of the task across environment changes @shindo2025.

== Large language models as a source of symbolic knowledge

The third shift tackles the oldest weakness in symbolic AI. Someone has to write the rules. Large language models can now produce rules, programs, and specifications from natural language descriptions. BlendRL's logic policies are generated by prompting an LLM with descriptions of the environment and example rules, not by hand @shindo2025. Code as Policies uses an LLM to write executable robot control programs straight from instructions, so the policy itself is a program @liang2023. SymbolicAI turns this into a framework where LLMs act as semantic parsers that translate instructions into operations sent to symbolic solvers @dinu2024.

The relationship runs the other way too. RLSF uses symbolic tools like compilers and solvers to give reward to an LLM that's being fine-tuned with RL. Those tools return certificates that pinpoint errors, so they give fine-grained and sound feedback where a learned reward model would give you one noisy score @jha2024. Yang et al. survey the broader convergence of neurosymbolic methods and LLM reasoning @yang2025llm. For neurosymbolic RL, the key point is that LLMs lower the cost of getting symbolic knowledge, but they don't guarantee it's correct. @sec:open takes up that tension.

== Symbolic constraints for safety and adaptation

The fourth shift uses symbolic parts to enforce constraints that pure RL can only encourage. Probabilistic logic shields use ProbLog to compute the probability that each action is safe given uncertain perception. Then they reweight the policy toward safe actions while keeping it differentiable, so safety is enforced during training and at deployment @yang2023. Neurosymbolic action masking takes a related route. A neural network maps states to symbolic models represented as probabilistic sentential decision diagrams. Those assign zero probability to states that violate domain constraints, and they're used to mask infeasible actions. Han et al. report near-zero constraint violations across four constrained domains, including Sudoku and N-Queens, plus better sample efficiency @han2026.

Khandelwal et al. take the idea from fixed constraints to changing ones @khandelwal2026. They argue that when a safety rule or operating requirement changes, you shouldn't retrain the agent. Instead, the agent should consult an external, editable knowledge graph of rules, hard constraints, and soft preferences. Then a change in requirements becomes an edit to the graph, enforced right away through action shielding and preference-based shaping. Their paper is a position paper that lays out an evaluation protocol rather than reporting experiments. But it points to a practical gap. Conventional RL has no adaptation mechanism that's both immediate and auditable.

== The return of answer set programming

The fifth shift is smaller. Answer set programming is getting renewed use, especially where rules have to be learned from few examples. DUA learns an ASP meta-policy with ILASP from one training arena per task category @mitchener2022. ILASP has also been used to learn reward-machine-like automata from traces @furelosblanco2021. SR-DQN, published in 2026, encodes partial policies learned on small instances of a task as ASP rules. It uses them to bias exploration and rescale the Q-values of a DQN agent on larger instances. The authors report better sample efficiency than reward-machine baselines and robustness to imperfect rules @veronese2026. These methods share an appeal. ASP rules are readable, they can express defaults and preferences directly, and they can be learned from very little data.

== Changing ways of organising the field

Finally, the field is changing how it describes itself. The three-class taxonomy from @yu2023 splits systems into learning for reasoning, reasoning for learning, and learning-reasoning. It classifies systems by the direction in which the two components help each other. Recent surveys organize things differently. Delvecchio et al. classify neurosymbolic methods by task, into rule mining, rule enforcement, and program synthesis @delvecchio2025. Colelough and Regli classify research by area and find that learning and inference dominate while explainability and meta-cognition stay underexplored @colelough2024. For RL specifically, a task-based view fits recent work well. Object-centric logic policies mine rules, shields and action masks enforce them, and LLM-generated policies synthesize programs. @sec:taxonomies compares these schemes in detail.